Booking a bed and breakfast in Amboise means choosing to stay just a few minutes’ drive from the most beautiful châteaux of the Loire Valley, in a region where life moves at a leisurely pace. At the Château de la Huberdière, you won’t be staying in an ordinary hotel: you’ll be staying in a 16th-century family home, with the warm welcome of a guesthouse, its enclosed grounds, its table d’hôte meals and the tranquillity of the Touraine countryside.

The château is situated in Nazelles-Négron, just across the Loire from Amboise. You’re just ten minutes from the Clos Lucé and the royal château, yet already far removed from the hustle and bustle. Here’s what sets a château with a guesthouse feel apart from ordinary accommodation, and how to plan your stay.

## The charm of a bed and breakfast, just a stone’s throw from Amboise

Our rooms retain the charm of a guest house: original wooden floors, high ceilings, and views of the grounds or the hillsides. The château has ten rooms and suites, each with its own en-suite bathroom, and no two are alike. One overlooks the centuries-old trees, another the rooftops, whilst a third faces due south. You’ll sleep soundly here, with the windows open to the tranquillity.

This variety is intentional. Here, no two rooms are the same: each has its own story, its own hand-picked furniture and its own chosen colour scheme. You don’t book a room category; you choose a specific room. To find out more about them, including their facilities and rates, please visit the [Stay at the Château](/en/sejour) page.

## What’s included in your stay at the château

It’s all down to the little details and the care we put into things. A stay with us involves much more than just a bed.

- Homemade breakfast, served every morning from 8.30 am to 10.30 am
- Access to the 14-hectare enclosed park and the heated swimming pool from April to October
- Free parking on the estate
- Wi-Fi and advice on itineraries for your visits
- Evening table d’hôte meals, by reservation only

Breakfast is a special time. Pastries, homemade jams, seasonal fruit and local produce: this is where you get ready for the day ahead and chat about the sights you shouldn’t miss. Many of our guests cherish this memory just as much as their memories of the castles.

## The château, between Amboise and the Loire Valley

La Huberdière is a 16th-century private estate, set within a walled park full of trees. Here, you can hear the birds, not the road. This is exactly what people come to Touraine for when they dream of a guesthouse with character: an authentic, lived-in place, far removed from standardised accommodation.

The location is ideal for exploring the surrounding area. Amboise and its Sunday market, the vineyards of Vouvray and Montlouis, the cave-dwelling villages – everything is just a few minutes’ drive away. You set off in the morning to explore, and return in the evening to your own grounds, without ever having to drive for long.

## A home-cooked meal

In the evenings, by reservation, I prepare a table d’hôtes using produce from the market and the vegetable garden. As an Italian by birth, I enjoy blending the cuisine of the Touraine region with that of my childhood. We dine together, around a single table, and the conversations often go on late into the night.

It’s a different way of travelling: meeting the people who run the place, sampling home-cooked food, and leaving with a recommendation rather than an anonymous booking. We can cater for vegetarian or gluten-free diets – just let us know in advance. Full details of our catering are on the [catering](/en/restauration) page.

## How much does a bed and breakfast cost in Amboise?

The price per night at the château depends on the room, the season and the length of your stay. The rate always includes a home-made breakfast and access to the grounds and the swimming pool. Rather than a fixed price, we prefer to show actual availability and let you choose the room that suits you best. You can view the latest rates directly on the [accommodation](/en/sejour) page.

For the same price, a château like ours offers what a traditional hotel cannot: the space of a park, peace and quiet, home-cooked meals, and the feeling of being welcomed rather than simply accommodated.

## When to visit Touraine

Every season has its own character. Spring brings the valley’s gardens to life – Villandry and Chaumont-sur-Loire in particular – with soft light and few visitors. Summer is the perfect time for the swimming pool and long evenings in the park. Autumn paints the Vouvray vineyards in vibrant colours at harvest time. Winter, finally, has the charm of absolute tranquillity and a log fire, ideal for a weekend for two.

There’s no such thing as a bad time to visit – just different atmospheres. Tell us what you’re looking for, and we’ll advise you on the best season and what to see.

## Things to do around Amboise

The Loire Valley boasts a concentration of châteaux that is unrivalled in France. From La Huberdière, you can reach the most famous ones in half an hour. To plan your visits, read our guide to [visiting the châteaux of the Loire](/en/blog/visiter-chateaux-de-la-loire).

A few points of interest in the immediate vicinity:

- the Royal Château of Amboise and the Clos Lucé, Leonardo da Vinci’s final resting place
- Chenonceau, the château on the River Cher, twenty minutes away
- the Vouvray wine cellars for the tasting
- cycling along the banks of the Loire, on the Loire à Vélo route
- Chaumont-sur-Loire and its garden festival, twenty-five minutes away

## Book your stay

Whether you’re staying for a night, a weekend or a week, the Château de la Huberdière welcomes you all year round, offering the warmth of a guest house. Check availability and details on the [accommodation](/en/sejour) page, or write to us directly to arrange your stay.

Are you looking for a charming place to stay in Amboise where you can explore the châteaux of the Loire Valley at your own pace? You’ve found it.
